    "field": "Sports / Exercise Physiology",
    "plotType": "Linear F-V line with overlaid parabolic power curve",
    "description": "The sprint force-velocity-power profile plots the horizontal force an athlete produces against their running velocity, yielding a downward-sloping straight line (the F-V relationship) whose intercepts give theoretical maximal force F0 and maximal velocity V0. The product of force and velocity forms an inverted-U power curve that peaks at intermediate speeds (Pmax). Sprint coaches use it to classify whether an athlete is force-limited or velocity-limited.",
    "dataNeeded": [
      {
        "name": "Running velocity",
        "unit": "m·s⁻¹"
      },
      {
        "name": "Horizontal force",
        "unit": "N·kg⁻¹"
      },
      {
        "name": "Mechanical power",
        "unit": "W·kg⁻¹"
      }
    ],
    "whatItTellsYou": "A steep line with high F0 but low V0 marks a force-dominant athlete who should train top-end speed, whereas a shallow line with high V0 but low F0 marks a velocity-dominant athlete who should build strength; the goal is an individually balanced profile that maximizes Pmax.",
